<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry Rawson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Admiral Rawson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harry Rawson was a British admiral who commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Punitive Expedition</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> in 1897. The expedition followed the killing of a British delegation and resulted in the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, the looting of the palace and the exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a>. It ended the independence of the Benin Empire, and the looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> were dispersed abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-rawson.jpg" alt="Harry Rawson" width="288" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Freeman Brothers Studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Admiral_Sir_Harry_Rawson,_Sydney,_c._1902.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harry Holdsworth Rawson was born at Walton-on-Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, on 5 November 1843, the son of Christopher Rawson, of a landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> family. He was educated at Eastman's Royal Naval Academy and at Marlborough College, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in 1857. In October 1871 he married Florence Alice Stewart Shaw, daughter of John Ralph Shaw of Arrowe Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>; the couple had five children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rawson took part in the capture of the Taku Forts in 1860 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a>. Promoted to captain in 1877, he commanded HMS Minotaur and was Principal Transport Officer in the Anglo-Egyptian War of 1882. He became Flag Captain to the Commander-in-Chief of the Mediterranean Fleet in 1883 and captain of HMS Benbow in 1889.</p><p>In 1895 he was given command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> and West Coast of Africa Station. That year he oversaw the burning of Gongoro and the storming of Mweli, and from December 1895 to January 1896 he supported the Fourth Anglo-Asanti War, which led to the removal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prempeh-i">Prempeh I</a> of Asanteman. He commanded the British forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zanzibar-war">Anglo-Zanzibar War</a> of 27 August 1896, which lasted 38 minutes, and was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>.</p><p>In British circles the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> expedition of 1897, which he organised, was regarded as a feat of planning: a force of 1,200 men was landed and equipped in twenty-nine days, and Benin was taken five days later. From 1898 to April 1901 he commanded the Channel Squadron.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In February 1902 Rawson was appointed Governor of New South Wales, the first naval officer to hold the post since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a>. He arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> on 26 May 1902, took office the next day and served until 27 May 1909, his term having been extended. His wife died at sea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> on 3 December 1905 while returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Rawson died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 3 November 1910 after an operation for appendicitis. The Rawson Cup of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-sydney">University of Sydney</a>'s colleges, which he donated in 1906, Rawson House at Cranbrook School and the locality of Rawsonville near Dubbo bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harry Rawson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A naval punitive expedition under Admiral <b>Harry Rawson</b> sacked and destroyed Benin City and took the works decorating the palace and the nobles' residences.</blockquote><a href="/work/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1886" aria-label="Liverpool on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4483 327 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harry Rawson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4933 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4933 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1886">What was here in 1886? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
